Run a command with a `.env`'s variables in its environment.

The local `evnx cloud run`: the file is read and handed to the child process in its environment block, never its arguments, so `ps` shows the command and not the values, and the command never reads `.env` itself. evnx's own `EVNX_*` settings are not passed on.

The command runs directly, not through a shell. For a pipeline or a builtin, ask for one: `-- sh -c 'a | b'`. Exits with the command's code.

Usage: evnx run [OPTIONS] -- <COMMAND>...

Arguments:
  <COMMAND>...
          The command to run, after `--`

Options:
      --env <ENV>
          The file to read. Defaults to `.env`, or `[defaults] env_name`

      --env-name <NAME>
          Read `.env.<NAME>` instead of `.env`

      --include <GLOB>
          Inject only variables whose name matches one of these globs

      --exclude <GLOB>
          Skip variables whose name matches one of these globs

      --allow-unsafe-names
          Inject variables that make a program load or run other code (`PATH`, `LD_*`, `DYLD_*`, `NODE_OPTIONS`, `BASH_ENV`, …), refused by default

      --mask
          Mask secret values in the command's output as `***`.
          
          On by default when output is not a terminal (a CI log, an agent), where what is printed is kept. Only values the classifier calls secret (by name or by value) are masked. It prevents accidents, not exfiltration: a command that transforms a value gets past it.

      --no-mask
          Never mask the command's output

      --prefer <SIDE>
          Which side wins when a variable is set both in your environment and in the env file: `vault` (default) or `env`.
          
          `vault` lets the vault or env file replace what your environment holds (today's behaviour). `env` keeps a variable your environment already holds, even an empty one, and only fills the names it leaves unset: for testing with a local value over a shared one. A line on stderr names what was kept, never a value. Names are checked either way; a kept value is not masked, since evnx did not inject it.

          Possible values:
          - vault: The vault or env file wins: today's behaviour
          - env:   A variable already set in the environment wins (even when empty); the vault or file only fills names that are unset
          
          [default: vault]

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Which value wins: by default the env file replaces a variable your environment
already holds. --prefer env reverses that, so a local value is kept and the file
only fills the names you did not set. Testing with a local value:
  DB_URL=postgres://localhost evnx run --prefer env -- npm test

Signals and exit (Unix): without masking the command replaces evnx, so signals,
the terminal and the exit status are the command's own. With masking evnx stays
to filter the output and passes SIGTERM, SIGHUP, SIGQUIT and SIGINT on to the
command (not to what it starts), then exits with its code, or 128+n when signal
n killed it. Windows: no signal forwarding and no job object, so the command can
outlive evnx when the console closes; its exit code is passed through.

Exit codes:
    0  Ran, nothing to report
    2  Could not run: usage, config, I/O, or refused before any change
  141  Output closed early (broken pipe)
    N  Once the command has started: its own exit code, or 128+n if signal n killed it

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/run
